<mujoco model="helios">
  <!--
    Helios rover for RL training. Visually the same robot RViz shows, driven
    through the same interface the real rover exposes: a body twist on
    /cmd_vel, never individual wheels.

    TWO LAYERS, as in helios_description's URDF:
      visual     the CAD meshes, excluded from collision, cost nothing
      collision  one box matching Nav2's 0.38 x 0.38 footprint

    WHY NO WHEELS IN THE PHYSICS. A mecanum wheel's motion comes entirely
    from its rollers, which the URDF does not describe. Modelling four hinges
    without them would spin the wheels and move nothing. mecanum_kinematics.py
    already converts body twist to wheel commands downstream of /cmd_vel, so
    the sim takes that twist directly: slide x, slide y, hinge z. The wheel
    meshes are therefore decoration and do not turn.

    GEOMETRY SOURCE. Every number here is traceable to
    helios_description/urdf/. That package stays the one source of truth; a
    re-measurement there must be copied here. Mesh geom poses are the URDF's
    joint origin composed with its visual origin, since the four wheel links
    and two sensor links collapse into this one body.
  -->

  <compiler meshdir="meshes" angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <!--
    4 ms puts the integrator an order of magnitude above the 20 Hz control
    rate. implicitfast is stable with the stiff velocity actuators below,
    where Euler needs a far smaller step.
  -->
  <option timestep="0.004" integrator="implicitfast"/>

  <default>
    <!--
      Visual geoms carry contype and conaffinity 0, so they never enter
      collision detection. Measured: 202,102 steps/s with all four meshes
      loaded versus 194,694 for the bare box, i.e. free.
    -->
    <default class="visual">
      <geom type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2" density="0"/>
    </default>
    <default class="collision">
      <geom group="3" rgba="0.9 0.4 0.1 0.25"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <!-- Colours copied from helios_description/urdf/common.xacro. -->
    <material name="chassis_grey" rgba="0.30 0.32 0.35 1.0"/>
    <material name="wheel_black" rgba="0.10 0.10 0.10 1.0"/>
    <material name="sensor_blue" rgba="0.10 0.30 0.80 1.0"/>
    <material name="floor_grey" rgba="0.42 0.44 0.46 1.0"/>

    <!--
      Decimated by tools/decimate_meshes.py: MuJoCo rejects any STL over
      200 k faces and the chassis export is 1.66 M. scale converts the CAD
      millimetres to metres.
    -->
    <mesh name="chassis" file="Rover-Chassis.STL" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="wheel_a" file="A4WD3-W-ME-A.STL" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="wheel_b" file="A4WD3-W-ME-B.STL" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="lidar" file="Hokuyo-UST-10LX-Lidar.STL" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="zed" file="ZED2i.STL" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 4" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>

    <!--
      The floor is decoration. The body has no vertical freedom, so it can
      never rest on anything, and a colliding plane here just pins four
      permanent contacts under the footprint box for the solver to chew on
      every step. Obstacles are the things that need to collide.
      Re-enable collision here if the base ever gains a free joint.
    -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="20 20 0.1" material="floor_grey"
          contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!--
      base_link sits at wheel-axle height, 0.076 m up, matching the URDF. The
      three joints below give no vertical freedom, so the body cannot fall or
      tip and never touches the floor.
    -->
    <body name="base_link" pos="0 0 0.076">
      <!--
        Only izz is ever used: a planar body cannot roll or pitch. The URDF's
        0.10 agrees within 7% with the box formula m(L^2 + W^2)/12 for 7 kg at
        0.32 x 0.24 m, which gives 0.0933.

        MASS IS A PLACEHOLDER. 7.0 kg is the URDF's modelled total, 5.0 for
        the chassis plus 0.5 per wheel, and it counts neither the sensors
        (~0.54 kg) nor the battery. Weigh the rover and correct it. The
        training env should randomise it anyway: under the rate limits below,
        a 2.4x mass error moves the velocity step response by only 10%.
      -->
      <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="7.0" diaginertia="0.05 0.08 0.10"/>

      <joint name="slide_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0"/>
      <joint name="slide_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0"/>
      <joint name="hinge_z" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1"/>

      <!--
        The only colliding geom. 0.19 half-extents match navigation_pkg's
        footprint, which is the span across the WHEELS (0.38 x 0.38), not the
        0.32 x 0.24 chassis. Obstacle clearance has to respect the wheels.
        Half-height is the wheel radius, so the box spans the rover's real
        vertical extent.
      -->
      <geom class="collision" name="footprint" type="box" size="0.19 0.19 0.076"/>

      <!-- Visual: chassis. quat 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5 is the URDF's shared
           mesh_rpy of (pi/2, 0, pi/2), which maps the CAD axes to REP-103. -->
      <geom class="visual" mesh="chassis" material="chassis_grey"
            pos="-0.16552 -0.13383 -0.03827" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>

      <!-- Visual: wheels. Mecanum wheels are handed, so diagonal corners
           share a mesh. Mesh choice and offsets follow base.xacro exactly. -->
      <geom class="visual" mesh="wheel_b" material="wheel_black"
            pos="0.02602 0.13667 -0.08436" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>
      <geom class="visual" mesh="wheel_a" material="wheel_black"
            pos="0.02605 -0.19333 -0.08436" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>
      <geom class="visual" mesh="wheel_a" material="wheel_black"
            pos="-0.19395 0.13667 -0.08436" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>
      <geom class="visual" mesh="wheel_b" material="wheel_black"
            pos="-0.19398 -0.19333 -0.08436" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>

      <!-- Visual: sensors. The ZED mesh has its own CAD axes, hence a
           different quat: the URDF's (0, 0, pi/2). -->
      <geom class="visual" mesh="lidar" material="sensor_blue"
            pos="0.01837 -0.025 0.065" quat="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5"/>
      <geom class="visual" mesh="zed" material="sensor_blue"
            pos="0.12147 -0.08888 0.02872" quat="0.70710678 0 0 0.70710678"/>

      <!--
        Raycast origins, at the URDF's measured mount points. laser is where
        mj_multiRay emits the scan from, so it carries slam_toolbox's
        scan-matching lever arm; it must stay in step with laser_mount_joint.
      -->
      <site name="laser" pos="0.05 0 0.100" size="0.01" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>
      <site name="zed_mount" pos="0.106 0 0.050" size="0.01" rgba="0 0 1 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    Velocity actuators, one per axis, deliberately stiff. On the real rover
    acceleration is capped in SOFTWARE by Nav2's velocity_smoother, not by
    motors fighting inertia, so the env applies that same rate limit to the
    command (accel 0.80/0.80/2.00, decel -0.50/-0.50/-1.50) and the actuator
    only has to track it.

    kv sets a first-order lag of tau = mass / kv. These are tuned so that lag
    is negligible against the rate-limited ramp, measured as residual lag
    behind the ramp for a full-scale step on each axis:

        kv lin/rot      vx        vy        wz
           200/40    +37.0ms   +65.2ms    0.0ms
           400/80    +17.0ms   +25.3ms    0.0ms
           800/160    +5.0ms    +9.3ms    0.0ms     <- chosen
          2000/400    +1.0ms    +1.3ms    0.0ms

    vy is the axis that exposes a soft actuator, because its 0.10 m/s step
    ramps in 119 ms and a 35 ms lag is a third of that. The deliberate choice
    is a near-ideal actuator here and real command latency modelled
    explicitly in the env instead: the hardware's delay is transport lag
    through serial, the RoboClaw PID and the EKF, which is a delay buffer and
    not a first-order lag, and it needs to be randomisable.

    ctrlrange is the real envelope from navigation_pkg/config/nav2_params.yaml.
    vy is 0.10 and not 0.40 on purpose: mecanum rollers lose far more to slip
    sideways than forward, so the rover drives nose-first and strafes only as
    a fine correction. Training with a free 0.40 m/s strafe teaches a policy
    to crab in a way the hardware cannot follow.
  -->
  <actuator>
    <velocity name="vx" joint="slide_x" kv="800" ctrlrange="-0.35 0.40"/>
    <velocity name="vy" joint="slide_y" kv="800" ctrlrange="-0.10 0.10"/>
    <velocity name="wz" joint="hinge_z" kv="160" ctrlrange="-0.80 0.80"/>
  </actuator>
</mujoco>
